  "result": "`summarize()` in `src/report.js` is now fast. The 200,000-order test finishes well inside its 1-second limit. `npm test` passes, 5 of 5.\n\n**What was slow**\n- Each order did a linear scan to check for a duplicate id.\n- Each order also did a linear scan to find its customer's group.\n- The whole leaderboard was re-sorted after every order.\n\n**What changed**\n- Duplicate ids are tracked in a `Set` and customer groups in a `Map`.\n- The leaderboard is sorted once at the end. `byRank` is a total order (the customer id breaks every tie), so the final order is identical to sorting after each order.\n- Refunded orders still count as \"seen\" for de-duplication, and the keys in each result object keep their order. Nothing else in the logic was touched.\n\n**Checking the output is unchanged**\n- I ran the original function from git against the new one on 200 random datasets with duplicates, refunds, EUR/USD, ties and varying limits. The results were deeply equal every time. That script lives in the scratchpad and is not part of the repo.\n- Three tests were added to `test/report.test.js`:\n  - A comparison against an independent reference implementation on a mixed dataset. It also checks the key order of the result objects.\n  - Duplicate ids where a refunded copy arrives first, plus the tie-break order for customers with equal spend.\n  - The 200,000-order timing check.\n\nOne edge case I didn't handle: a `NaN` customer id would group differently under `Map` than under the old `===` lookup. Order data shouldn't contain that.",
